[Music]  [Applause]  this is what the world has come to  expect from australia  voted one of the greatest pyrotechnic  shows on earth  but the upcoming new year's eve  fireworks spectacular was almost snuffed  out  fresh outbreaks of coronavirus forcing  new lockdowns  in the same way last year when we had  the bushfires there was a call to cancel  the fireworks for exactly  you know similar kinds of reasons not so  much that you couldn't gather on the  harbour's edge but  was it appropriate and overwhelming the  feedback that we got from a lot of  regional communities was  no we need those fireworks to be there  it's a sign of normality it's a sign of  hope they need to happen  final preparations and last minute  checks to ensure the show goes on  without a hitch  the foti family has been putting on  fireworks displays  since 1793 more than 20 in sydney  but this year's show will be cut back to  just  seven minutes it's more of an intimate  display  but the intensity i would say would  probably be more than what it's been in  previous years  ordinarily viewing areas just like this  would already be jam-packed  but not in a covered year the one to one  and a half  million people who cram every spare inch  of the sydney harbour foreshore to watch  the fireworks  are this year banned that means sydney  ciders  like the rest of the world will have to  watch their fireworks  on television miles and miles of high  stool barricades block any access to the  viewing areas  right across the city people living on  the harbour  will have a bird's eye view but they're  virtually the only ones  still tourism leaders already seeing  four billion dollars lost  every month hope tonight's fireworks are  the first steps  towards financial recovery from this  worldwide epidemic  little wonder these fireworks are seen  as such a sign of hope  david richardson sky news australia

